---

## THE SHAPE OF THIS CHAPTER IS DIFFERENT

Chapters 3–6 are theory: definitions, theorems, proofs. Chapter 7 is an APPLICATION
chapter, and most of its text is narrative — how a shift cipher works, why factoring
is hard, Alice and Bob, a worked RSA example with p = 23, q = 29. Only two results
are theorems at all:

- §7.1: an affine cipher `c = ap + b` is decodable exactly when `a` is a unit, with
  `f⁻¹(c) = a⁻¹c − a⁻¹b`.
- §7.2: RSA's round trip — `y^D = x^{DE} = x^{kφ(n)+1} = (x^{φ(n)})^k·x = x`.

Both are PROVED (`std/integer/mod-n.b4m`'s `anAffineCipherWithAUnitMultiplierDecodes`
and `std/group/order-divides.b4m`'s
`anExponentOneAboveAMultipleOfTheOrderIsTheIdentityMap`). So the chapter needs very
little new machinery — which is itself the finding, and the reason this file is short
next to Chapter 6's.

## STILL MISSING

### RSA's correctness is stated over a GROUP, not over ℤ_n — the gcd ≠ 1 case is unreachable

Judson's proof splits:
- `gcd(x, n) = 1` — then x is a unit, Euler applies, and the round trip is the group
  fact that IS proved.
- `gcd(x, n) ≠ 1` — "since n = pq and x < n, we know x is a multiple of p or a
  multiple of q, but not both", then CRT finishes it.

The second case is not a group statement at all: it needs n's FACTORIZATION as pq,
the Chinese Remainder Theorem, and `x < n`. None of the three exists in std. So what
is proved is RSA-for-units, which is the case the chapter actually computes with, and
the other case is recorded as prose.

The honest framing: the group fact is the mathematics; the second case is an artifact
of RSA being stated over all of ℤ_n rather than over U(n).

### No φ FUNCTION, so RSA's exponent arithmetic cannot be stated as the book writes it

`DE ≡ 1 (mod m)` where `m = φ(n) = (p−1)(q−1)` is the key relation, and the proof
needs `DE = kφ(n) + 1`. The proved theorem takes the group's size as a bare `Int`
parameter `m` with `power(g, m) = E` as a premise, which is the same content — but
`φ` itself does not exist, so the `m = (p−1)(q−1)` step cannot be written.

THIS IS CHAPTER 6'S GAP, INHERITED, and `CHAPTER6-PAINPOINTS` records it from the other
side. U(n)'s listing landed, and Euler with it, but φ AS A FUNCTION was never built.
φ(n) would be the LENGTH of the units' listing
(`mod_n_listing.znSplitsAtUnits`), and defining a FUNCTION from it
needs listing-length uniqueness transported to U(n)
(`lagrange.anyTwoGroupListingsHaveTheSameLength` exists for the group; it has not been
transferred). Nothing here is new mathematics; the function is simply not built.

### `addInverseRight` is not stated over ℤ_n (only the LEFT form)

`std/integer/mod-n.b4m` has `addInverseLeft: add(neg(x), x) = ZERO` and no right
counterpart, though `addCommutes` and `addIdentityRight` both exist. The
affine proof needs the right form and derives it inline in three steps
(commute + chain). A one-line theorem next to `addInverseLeft` would have saved it;
left as an observation rather than added, since the same asymmetry may be deliberate
elsewhere in the file.

Worth checking whether the ℤ_n ring surface has other one-sided-only laws — this one
was found by tripping over it, not by audit.

### Matrices (the polyalphabetic cipher) are out of scope, as recorded in Chapter 4

§7.1's last construction encrypts PAIRS of letters with an invertible 2×2 matrix over
ℤ_26. There is no matrix theory in std, and the user ruled matrices out of scope
during Chapter 4. Recorded as prose.

### Nothing in the chapter needed a new ENGINE feature

Unlike Chapters 5 and 6, no entry here is about the checker. Both proofs went through
first try on the existing primitives (`specialize`, `rewrite`, `chain`, `symmetry`)
with no accelerant surprises, no model gymnastics and no schema work. The friction was
entirely MISSING LIBRARY CONTENT (φ, congruence, CRT, one-sided ℤ_n laws), which is a
much better place for it to be.
